 the statements were  true and that they were not made with  actual mouse she argued that she had the  right to rely on jones's original claims  now cardi b's first and main claim was  for defamation we've covered defamation  many times but you generally prove that  by showing four elements a false  statement was made about the plaintiff  the statement was published where other  people saw or heard it the statement  harmed the plaintiff's reputation and no  privilege or defense existed for the  statement and georgia law basically  tracks our basic understanding of  defamation and of course you have to  have this certain mental state generally  it's negligence about the falsity of the  statements but when you're dealing with  someone like a celebrity you have to  have actual mouse which means you  knowingly or recklessly said these false  things that's the higher standard  doesn't have anything to do with  actually not liking a person actual  mouse is about knowingly or recklessly  repeating these false statements and of  course truth is a defense to defamation  in other words defamatory statements  must be false to be actionable you can  say something mean about someone that  hurts their reputation as long as what  you say is accurate or it's you know  opinion or other things that don't rise  to the level of defamation and here  there was no question that the  statements were published and  disseminated to third parties but the  questions of whether a particular  communication was defamatory and whether  the defendant had the requisite mental  state were for the jury to decide now  here the mental state is really  interesting because usually you only  have to prove negligence but following  in a famous supreme court case new york  times versus sullivan when you're  dealing with a public figure you have to  prove actual malice in the sullivan case  the supreme court held that public  official could not recover damages for  defamatory statements regarding his or  her official conduct unless it was  proven that the statement was made with  actual malice which again is a legal  term of art that has nothing to do with  hatred of a person it's about the mental  state of knowing that the the statements  are false later the supreme court  extended the actual malice rule to apply  to criticism of a public figure public  figures are people who are intimately  involved in the resolution of important  public questions or by reason of their  fame shape events in areas of concern to  society at large basically the supreme  court thought that if it was too easy to  prove defamation then rich and  influential people as well as government  officials could use their power to chill  free speech and that could certainly be  a concern in a case like this since an  extremely famous person who presumably  has lots of money  sued someone who has presumably fewer  resources and this is why tasha k called  out cardi b for attempting to silence  her voice as a blogger on the other hand  vloggers like tasha k have pretty big  platforms to spread malicious gossip and  these days everyone has access to the  internet where these things can be  repeated so her success in social media  made her a bigger target than someone  who doesn't have a big social media  presence now this wasn't necessarily an  easy case the judge in cardi b's case  concluded that there was a genuine  dispute of material fact about whether  all of tasha k's statements were in fact  false and defamatory and that it was  going to be up to the jury to decide  these questions for example in this case  cardi denied ever being a prostitute or  using drugs however tasha k offered  evidence that during interviews cardi  had talked about engaging in sexual acts  in exchange for money in that same video  cardi b discusses her use of drugs and  about having to pop pills sometimes when  necessary what does it mean to pop pills  does this imply that she popped cocaine  and molly or that she took benadryl and  tylenol the jury's job was to review all  the evidence and decide whether these  statements were false so let's talk  about the evidence regarding stis juries  who hear civil cases review quite a lot  of evidence and here's a list of the  exhibits submitted in to the court in  this particular case there are 1021  exhibits now the jury didn't hear hours  of testimony on each of these exhibits  but they did have access to all this  material which card to be alleged proved  the elements of her claims and here's  tasha k's list of exhibits she offered  125 exhibits in an attempt to prove that  what she said was true about cardi b and  although we aren't privy to the jury's  deliberations we do know that tasha  failed to prove that her comment about  cardi b having stis was true in  discovery tasha asked for an order  requiring cardi to turn over her medical  records cardi had already submitted sti  tests but did not fight the order the  judge granted her request to produce  material under protective order that  means that the judge can see the medical  records but that they are sealed from  public view and when you're talking  about medical records that makes perfect  sense and in december the judge reviewed  cardi's medical records and ruled that  they did not show that cardi had any  stis  the court has received and reviewed the  records and finds that they are not  helpful to defend it accordingly the  court will file the records under seal  at plaintiff's request only the court  will release the records to both sides  to be used at trial otherwise the record  shall not be opened except with  permission of the district judge or the  court of appeals and then there's  evidence about lying under oath why  didn't tasha kay have more credibility  with the jury well she was caught lying  on the stand on cross-examination tasha  k was asked isn't it a fact that you  testified in your deposition that you  were not a journalist answer yes so when  you told your subscribers that you were  a journalist you were lying answer yes  and when tasha told her viewers that her  lawyer friend had reviewed cardi's  season desist letter and that it was  bogus she was also lying under  cross-examination she said that the  person was just a friend not a lawyer  this by the way is called impeachment of  a witness and it can be incredibly  devastating she was also asked isn't it  true that you lied in your deposition  when you provided a date when you put up  a video defaming my client answer i made  up a date because you were being  combative towards me tasha's husband  also kind of threw her under the bus  asked is it true your wife lied when she  said that there are several businesses  under kb studios answer yes  journalist dennis byron a contributor to  tmz live tweeted the trial he revealed  that tasha's husband refuted her  testimony four times and then of course  there is the evidence about actual  malice here the jury must have found  that tashike acted with actual mouse  that is that she made statements with  knowledge that it was false or with  reckless disregard of whether it was  false or not what evidence did the jury  have to show that tasha  shouldn't have relied on jones and the  other statements well cardi's lawyers  had videos where tasha conceded that she  repeated things that she knew  weren't really true in one of the  exhibits a phone interview tasha states  here's my thing all of these strippers  are lying referring to jones cardi and a  third party in that same call tasha  concludes that jones quote told some  truths she didn't tell all truths one of  the most damning pieces of evidence in  this trial was when the court played a  phone call between tasha and celebrity  blogger lovely tea who told tasha that  the information from star marie jones  was fake and she shouldn't repeat the  claims at one point in the trial tasha  conceded that in her deposition she said  it would be funny to claim that cardi b  had hpv quote is it a fact that you  testified in your deposition that you  thought it was funny to say cardi b had  hpv answer yes that definitely does not  put tasha in a very sympathetic light  the jury also found in cardi's favor on  the invasion of privacy and intentional  infliction of emotional distress claims  in georgia a claim for intentional  infliction of emotional distress or ied  has four elements that there was an  intentional or reckless conduct which is  extreme and outrageous  which caused emotional distress which is  severe in support of her ied claim cardi  pointed to a video where tasha says she  had a personal vendetta against cardi  the jury could have found that tasha  therefore had a motive to defame cardi's  character in this case the jury was  probably satisfied that tasha's  statements were extreme and outrageous  however it's possible that this  particular verdict might get overturned  because conduct that can be  characterized as merely vulgar tasteless  rude or insulting generally should not  support a claim for ied now there was  also a cause of action for invasion of  privacy which ironically was the name of  cardi b's debut album georgia has  adopted four forms of invasion of  privacy the one at issue in this case is  invasion of privacy through portrayal in  a false light the elements of that claim  are that the defendant publicized a  matter concerning plaintiff that places  plaintiff in a false light the defendant  acts in a reckless disregard as to the  truth or falsity of the publicized  matter and the false light in which the  the plaintiff would be placed and the  the false light in which the plaintiff  was placed would be highly offensive to  a reasonable person and in support of  her invasion of privacy claim cardi b  emphasized the many times natasha  admitted that her stories were false  when she published them she apparently  was quoted as saying you know what's  funny i make up stories out of thin air  tasha just makes up stories 